## Architecture

```
src/{{ python_package }}/
├── application/          # Application bootstrap and DI wiring
├── domain/
│   ├── model/            # Entities, value objects (Pydantic v2)
│   └── usecase/          # Business logic, async use cases
└── infrastructure/
    ├── driven_adapters/  # Outbound adapters (DB, HTTP, secrets)
    ├── entry_points/     # Inbound adapters (REST, CLI, events)
    └── helpers/          # Cross-cutting utilities

tests/                    # Mirrors src/ structure
```

## Dependency injection

This project uses **wireup** with an async container (`application/config/wiring.py`).

- **Use cases** depend only on domain gateway ABCs. Add wiring with `scaffold guc --uses <gateway>`.
- **Driven adapters** implement gateways: `scaffold gda --gateway <gateway>`. Adapters may declare `<Name>Settings` (pydantic-settings) for configuration.
- **Helpers** are `@injectable` in infrastructure only—they must not be injected into use cases or domain. Use a gateway port if a use case needs a technical capability.
- **Entry points** use `Injected[UseCase]` or container resolution; do not import the application layer from infrastructure.

### Async-first and resources

- Prefer async I/O in adapters and entry points. The container validates the graph at startup.
- Adapters that own a long-lived resource (HTTP client, pool, etc.) should read configuration from generated `*Settings` modules (pydantic-settings) and register shared clients in `wiring.py` when needed. Settings without defaults fail fast when env vars are missing.
- Calling blocking libraries from async code: use `asyncio.to_thread(...)` rather than blocking the event loop.

### Scoped (per-request) dependencies

wireup supports scoped lifetimes, but this scaffold does not generate them. Add `scoped=…` on `@injectable` manually if you need per-request instances.

